{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788369191,"nonce":"HO+Hv3p9jhnhIackgS1aMaYCcaY=","message":"Implemented on `fix/e440676-error-class`, commit `56e986c`. **Not run against a bench** — builds clean (`dotnet build -c Release`, 0/0), no assembly on the branch. `RefusalStreak`'s pure logic is checked offline **18/18** against a verbatim copy of the shipped file with stubs for the three symbols it touches, the method `4e44116` used for `VerbArgs`.\n\n**Counts confirmed from the transcripts**: `build` refused `bad-args` 241 times, of which **238 are byte-identical** — `{\"def\":\"DeepDrill\",\"at\":\"122,130\",\"stuff\":\"Steel\"}` — plus 3 `Grave` calls that differ only in `at`.\n\n**THE MEASUREMENT THAT DECIDED THE DESIGN, and it is not in the issue: the 238 were NEVER ADJACENT.** Not once in 4,599 steps. Every one sat inside its own turn, between a `things` and a `journal`, in a fixed loop — `things → build → journal → advance → digest`. A streak counter over consecutive ENVELOPES would have read 1 every time and published nothing.\n\n**§1 — the shape.** The streak is per `(op, code, normalised args)` as asked; the TABLE is keyed by **op**, with the entry holding the code and argument fingerprint the streak is running on. That is what makes §3's reset rules fall out for free, bounds the table without an eviction policy, and needs no scan to clear an op's rows. The cost is measured and accepted: a caller alternating two doomed calls to one verb builds no streak, and DeepDrill reaches **94** rather than 238 under this keying because other `build` calls interrupted it. Under-reporting, never over-reporting — 94 is not a number anybody reads as normal. The **detail** is deliberately not part of the key: `busy`'s detail carries the in-flight advance's ticks-done and differs every call, so keying on it would mean the counter could never fire on `busy` (200 of the run's 691).\n\n**§2 — the threshold is 3, in calls-of-the-same-verb, which on this bench is TURNS** — and the never-adjacent measurement above is what proves the unit rather than assuming it. A turn on m1-20260901 was a mean 12,525 ticks of advance, so 3 ≈ fifteen in-game hours during which nothing about the refusal changed. Not 2: a second identical call is an ordinary retry, and `busy` is `flow` — *meant* to be retried. Not 10: the wedges that actually happened reached 94 and 60, so 3–20 all catch them and the smaller number catches a NEW one sooner.\n\n**§3 — resets** on a success of that verb, on any change to the arguments, on a different error code, and at a game boundary (`Runtime.ResetForGameBoundary`, beside `Placements` / `Layouts` / `ColonySampler`, for the same reason — a load can move `TicksGame` backward). A **per-code notion of which game state each code depends on is deliberately not modelled**: it would be the table beside the codes that `e440676`'s design refused an hour earlier, and its failure mode is silencing the field on the wedge it exists to name. `repeated.ticks` carries the honest signal instead.\n\n**A SECOND WEDGE IN THE SAME RUN, not in either issue.** Sixty consecutive `advance {until:{letter:true}, timeout_ticks:60000}` calls, every one refused `unread-journal` with a byte-identical detail, `m1-20260901-s02/695-advance` → `-s03/047-advance`. `TicksGame` 3,704,384 at the first and 3,704,384 at the last: five minutes of wall clock, sixty turns, **zero in-game ticks**.\n\nI nearly filed this as a mod defect and it is not one. The loop DID call `journal` between every pair — as `journal {since_seq:0, limit:2000}`, which exhausts its limit nine rows short of the tail, and a truncated read only moves the watermark as far as the rows it handed over (documented, `rwa/README.md`). Every reply published `count: 2000, read_watermark: 2024, **unread_after: 9**` and the loop advanced anyway. The blocking rows were seq 2025..2028 — `death 1, letter 3`, a colonist death the run never read, which is `722c951`'s founding case exactly. **The envelope carried the answer twice a turn and nobody read it sixty times**, which is this issue's argument in one sentence. Under the shipped counter it is named on turn three with `ticks: 0`.\n\n**§4** — `checklists/turn.md` gains `repeated-refusal`, with that diagnosis in the `unread-journal` branch (read `unread_after`; page from the last watermark, not from 0) and the escalation rule: a count in double figures beside `ticks: 0` is a wedge, escalate rather than take another turn.\n\n**§5** — `accept/f08dfc4-repeated-refusal.md`, ten `rwa` calls with one expectation each, per the round's no-Python-suite rule. The cockpit also puts `repeated` at the top of the fold, warned, above `error.code` — it is the one thing on a correctly-refused envelope that is alarming.\n\n**Not demonstrated**: nothing was dispatched through a live poller. No `repeated` block has ever been written to a result file, and the threshold has never been crossed on a real bench.","files":null}]}